.TH DP9IK 2
.SH NAME
dp9ik, tlspsk, rconnect \- 9front's authentication, its TLS, and rcpu's connection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "dp9ik.m";
dp := load Dp9ik Dp9ik->PATH;

init:          fn(): string;
passtoaeskey:  fn(password: array of byte): array of byte;
authpakhash:   fn(aeskey: array of byte, user: string): array of byte;
Pak.new:       fn(pakhash: array of byte, isclient: int, x: array of byte): ref Pak;
Pak.finish:    fn(p: self ref Pak, y: array of byte): array of byte;
sealticket:    fn(t: ref Ticket, key: array of byte): array of byte;
openticket:    fn(a: array of byte, key: array of byte): (ref Ticket, string);
sealauth:      fn(a: ref Authenticator, key: array of byte): array of byte;
openauth:      fn(b: array of byte, key: array of byte): (ref Authenticator, string);
sessionsecret: fn(rand: array of byte, tkey: array of byte): array of byte;

include "tlspsk.m";
tlspsk := load Tlspsk Tlspsk->PATH;
client: fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, identity: string, psk: array of byte): (ref Sys->FD, string);

include "rconnect.m";
rconnect := load Rconnect Rconnect->PATH;
connect: fn(host, keyspec, script: string): (ref Sys->FD, ref Factotum->Authinfo, string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Dp9ik
is what a client of 9front's authentication needs of 9front's
.IR authsrv (2),
written from its source and checked against answers 9front gives.
.B Passtoaeskey
is the AES key of a password (PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1, 9001 rounds, 16 bytes).
.B Authpakhash
maps a user's key to two points of Ed448-Goldilocks
(HKDF-SHA256 salted with the user's name, then Elligator 2), 448 bytes.
.B Pak.new
starts one side of the SPAKE2-EE exchange from that hash
(with the private scalar
.I x
given, for known answers, or random), its public key, decaf-encoded, in
.BR y ;
.B finish
takes the other side's and gives the 32-byte shared key, or nil when the
other's is no point, once.
.B Sealticket
and
.B sealauth
make form1 tickets and authenticators, ChaCha20-Poly1305 under a 32-byte key with
the nonce the message type's signature and a counter;
.B openticket
and
.B openauth
open them, refusing one changed, cut, of the wrong length or of another type.
.B Sessionsecret
is the 256-byte secret both ends of a
.B dp9ik
conversation have (HKDF-SHA256 of the ticket key, salted with both nonces).
.B Ticketreq
packs and unpacks 9front's ticket request.
.PP
.B Tlspsk
is a TLS 1.2 client with a pre-shared key and nothing else
(RFC 4279 and RFC 7905:
.BR TLS_PSK_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256 ),
as 9front's
.I tlssrv
speaks it after
.BR p9any .
.B Client
makes the handshake over
.I fd
and returns one end of a pipe that is the conversation.
It is not
.IR tls (2),
offers no certificates, no other suite and no renegotiation, and is used for nothing else.
.PP
.B Rconnect
makes a connection to a 9front cpu server's
.B rcpu
service: it dials
.IR host ,
authenticates through
.IR factotum (4)
.RB ( proto=p9any ),
starts
.B tlspsk
with the secret (identity
.BR p9secret )
and sends
.I script
for the server's
.I rc
to run.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/dp9ik.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/tlspsk.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/rconnect.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/auth/factotum/proto/p9any.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rcpu (1),
.IR factotum (4)
.SH BUGS
Classical cryptography only, for working with 9front: not CNSA 2.0, and
refused in CNSA mode by everything that uses it.
.PP
The arithmetic is IPint's, which is not constant time.
The scalars are a conversation's own; the password's hash is mapped to the curve once a login.
An observer able to time a client's login closely might learn something of that hash;
9front's own is exposed the same way where its multiprecision code is.
